        {
          "number": "8",
          "title": "Daily Prayers",
          "entries": [
            {
              "id": "h8_e1",
              "label": "Morning Prayer",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "In the morning, when you get up, bless yourself with the holy cross and say:\nIn the name of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. Amen.\nThen, kneeling or standing, say the Creed and the Lord’s Prayer.\nIf you choose, you can also say this little prayer:\nI thank You, my Heavenly Father, through Jesus Christ, Your dear Son, that You have kept me this night from all harm and danger; and I pray that You would keep me this day also from sin and every evil, that all my doings and life may please You. For into Your hands I commend myself, my body and soul, and all things. Let Your holy angel be with me, that the evil foe may have no power over me. Amen.\nThen go to your work joyfully, singing a hymn, such as the Ten Commandments, or whatever your devotion suggests.",
              "proofs": []
            },
            {
              "id": "h8_e2",
              "label": "Evening Prayer",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "In the evening, when you go to bed, bless yourself with the holy cross and say:\nIn the name of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. Amen.\nThen, kneeling or standing, say the Creed and the Lord’s Prayer.\nIf you choose, you can also say this little prayer:\nI thank You, my Heavenly Father, through Jesus Christ, Your dear Son, that You have graciously kept me this day; and I pray You to forgive me all my sins where I have done wrong, and graciously keep me this night. For into Your hands I commend myself, my body and soul, and all things. Let Your holy angel be with me, that the evil foe may have no power over me. Amen.\nThen go to sleep promptly and cheerfully.\n\nHow the head of the family should teach his household to ask a blessing and return thanks",
              "proofs": []
            },
            {
              "id": "h8_e3",
              "label": "Asking a Blessing",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "The family shall go to the table reverently and with folded hands, and say:\nThe eyes of all look to You, O Lord, and You give them their food in due season. You open Your hand and satisfy the desires of every living thing. [Psalm 145:15-16]\nThen shall follow the Lord’s Prayer and this prayer:\nLord God, heavenly Father, bless us and these Your gifts which we receive from Your bountiful goodness, through Jesus Christ, our Lord. Amen.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Psalm.145_15-16",
                      "display": "Psalm 145:15-16"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "h8_e4",
              "label": "Returning Thanks",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Likewise also after the meal they shall say reverently and with folded hands:\nGive thanks to the Lord, for He is good, for His steadfast love endures forever. He gives food to all flesh. He gives to the beasts their food, and to the young ravens that cry. His delight is not in the strength of the horse, nor his pleasure in the legs of a man, but the Lord takes pleasure in those who fear him, in those who hope in his steadfast love. [Psalm 136:1,25; Psalm 147:9-11]\nThen shall follow the Lord’s Prayer and this prayer:\nWe thank You, Lord God, heavenly Father, for all Your benefits, who live and reign forever. Amen.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Psalm.136_1~25",
                      "display": "Psalm 136:1,25"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 2,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Psalm.147_9-11",
                      "display": "Psalm 147:9-11"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": "9",
          "title": "A Table of Household Responsibilities",
          "entries": [
            {
              "id": "h9_e1",
              "label": "To Bishops, Pastors, and Preachers",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Therefore an overseer must be above reproach, the husband of one wife, sober-minded, self-controlled, respectable, hospitable, able to teach, not a drunkard, not violent but gentle, not quarrelsome, not a lover of money. He must manage his own household well, with all dignity keeping his children submissive. 1 Timothy 3:2-4\n\nHe must not be a recent convert, or he may become puffed up with conceit and fall into the condemnation of the devil. 1 Timothy 3:6\n\nHe must hold firm to the trustworthy word as taught, so that he may be able to give instruction in sound doctrine and also to rebuke those who contradict it. Titus 1:9",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.Timothy.3_2-4",
                      "display": "1 Timothy 3:2-4"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.Timothy.3_6",
                      "display": "1 Timothy 3:6"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Titus.1_9",
                      "display": "Titus 1:9"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "h9_e2",
              "label": "What the Hearers Owe Their Pastors",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "The Lord commanded that those who proclaim the gospel should get their living by the gospel. 1 Corinthians 9:14\n\nOne who is taught the word must share all good things with the one who teaches. Do not be deceived: God is not mocked, for whatever one sows, that will he also reap. Galatians 6:6-7\n\nLet the elders who rule well be considered worthy of double honour, especially those who labour in preaching and teaching. For the Scripture says, “You shall not muzzle an ox when it treads out the grain,” and, “The labourer deserves his wages.” 1 Timothy 5:17-18\n\nWe ask you, brothers, to respect those who labour among you and are over you in the Lord and admonish you, and to esteem them very highly in love because of their work. Be at peace among yourselves. 1 Thessalonians 5:12-13\n\nObey your leaders and submit to them, for they are keeping watch over your souls, as those who will have to give an account. Let them do this with joy and not with groaning, for that would be of no advantage to you. Hebrews 13:17",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.Corinthians.9_14",
                      "display": "1 Corinthians 9:14"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Galatians.6_6-7",
                      "display": "Galatians 6:6-7"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.Timothy.5_17-18",
                      "display": "1 Timothy 5:17-18"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.Thessalonians.5_12-13",
                      "display": "1 Thessalonians 5:12-13"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Hebrews.13_17",
                      "display": "Hebrews 13:17"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "h9_e3",
              "label": "Of Civil Government",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Let every person be subject to the governing authorities. For there is no authority except from God, and those that exist have been instituted by God. Therefore whoever resists the authorities resists what God has appointed, and those who resist will incur judgement. For rulers are not a terror to good conduct, but to bad. Would you have no fear of the one who is in authority? Then do what is good, and you will receive his approval, for he is God’s servant for your good. But if you do wrong, be afraid, for he does not bear the sword in vain. For he is the servant of God, an avenger who carries out God’s wrath on the wrongdoer. Romans 13:1-4",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Romans.13_1-4",
                      "display": "Romans 13:1-4"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "h9_e4",
              "label": "Of Citizens",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Give to Caesar what is Caesar’s, and to God what is God’s. Matthew 22:21\n\nIt is necessary to submit to the authorities, not only because of possible punishment but also because of conscience. This is also why you pay taxes, for the authorities are God’s servants, who give their full time to governing. Give everyone what you owe him:\n\nIf you owe taxes, pay taxes; if revenue, then revenue; if respect, then respect; if honour, then honour. Romans 13:5-7\n\nI urge, then, first of all, that requests, prayers, intercession and thanksgiving be made for everyone—for kings and all those in authority, that we may live peaceful and quiet lives in all godliness and holiness. This is good and pleases God our Saviour. 1 Timothy 2:1-3\n\nRemind the people to be subject to rules and authorities, to be obedient, to be ready to do whatever is good. Titus 3:1\n\nSubmit yourselves for the Lord’s sake to every authority instituted among men: whether to the king, as the supreme authority, or to governors, who are sent by him to punish those who do wrong and to commend those who do right. 1 Peter 2:13-14",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Matthew.22_21",
                      "display": "Matthew 22:21"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Romans.13_5-7",
                      "display": "Romans 13:5-7"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.Timothy.2_1-3",
                      "display": "1 Timothy 2:1-3"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Titus.3_1",
                      "display": "Titus 3:1"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.Peter.2_13-14",
                      "display": "1 Peter 2:13-14"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "h9_e5",
              "label": "To Husbands",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Husbands, in the same way be considerate as you live with your wives, and treat them with respect as the weaker partner and as heirs with you of the gracious gift of life, so that nothing will hinder your prayers. 1 Peter 3:7\n\nHusbands, love your wives and do not be harsh with them. Colossians 3:19",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.Peter.3_7",
                      "display": "1 Peter 3:7"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Colossians.3_19",
                      "display": "Colossians 3:19"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "h9_e6",
              "label": "To Wives",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Wives, submit to your husbands as to the Lord. Ephesians 5:22\n\nThey were submissive to their own husbands, like Sarah, who obeyed Abraham and called him her master. You are her daughters if you do what is right and do not give way to fear. 1 Peter 3:5-6",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Ephesians.5_22",
                      "display": "Ephesians 5:22"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.Peter.3_5-6",
                      "display": "1 Peter 3:5-6"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "h9_e7",
              "label": "To Parents",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Fathers, do not exasperate your children; instead, bring them up in the training and instruction of the Lord. Ephesians 6:4",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Ephesians.6_4",
                      "display": "Ephesians 6:4"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "h9_e8",
              "label": "To Children",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Children, obey your parents in the Lord, for this is right. “Honour your father and your mother”—which is the first commandment with a promise—“that it may go well with you and that you may enjoy long life on the earth.” Ephesians 6:1-3",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Ephesians.6_1-3",
                      "display": "Ephesians 6:1-3"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "h9_e9",
              "label": "To Workers of All Kinds",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Slaves, obey your earthly masters with respect and fear, and with sincerity of heart, just as you would obey Christ. Obey them not only to win their favour when their eye is on you, but like slaves of Christ, doing the will of God from your heart. Serve wholeheartedly, as if you were serving the Lord, not men, because you know that the Lord will reward everyone for whatever good he does, whether he is slave or free. Ephesians 6:5-8",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Ephesians.6_5-8",
                      "display": "Ephesians 6:5-8"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "h9_e10",
              "label": "To Employers and Supervisors",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Masters, treat your slaves in the same way. Do not threaten them, since you know that he who is both their Master and yours is in heaven, and there is no favouritism with Him. Ephesians 6:9",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Ephesians.6_9",
                      "display": "Ephesians 6:9"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "h9_e11",
              "label": "To Youth",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Young men, in the same way be submissive to those who are older. All of you, clothe yourselves with humility toward one another, because, “God opposes the proud but gives grace to the humble.” Humble yourselves, therefore, under God’s mighty hand, that He may lift you up in due time. 1 Peter 5:5-6",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.Peter.5_5-6",
                      "display": "1 Peter 5:5-6"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "h9_e12",
              "label": "To Widows",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "The widow who is really in need and left all alone puts her hope in God and continues night and day to pray and to ask God for help. But the widow who lives for pleasure is dead even while she lives. 1 Timothy 5:5-6",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.Timothy.5_5-6",
                      "display": "1 Timothy 5:5-6"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "h9_e13",
              "label": "To Everyone",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "The commandments … are summed up in this one rule: “Love your neighbour as yourself.” Romans 13:9\n\nI urge … that requests, prayers, intercession and thanksgiving be made for everyone. 1 Timothy 2:1\n\nLet each his lesson learn with care, And all the household well shall fare.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Romans.13_9",
                      "display": "Romans 13:9"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.Timothy.2_1",
                      "display": "1 Timothy 2:1"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": "10",
          "title": "Christian Questions with Their Answers",
          "entries": [
            {
              "id": "h10_1",
              "label": "1",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Do you believe that you are a sinner?\nYes, I believe it: I am a sinner.",
              "proofs": []
            },
            {
              "id": "h10_2",
              "label": "2",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "How do you know this?\nFrom the Ten Commandments, which I have not kept.",
              "proofs": []
            },
            {
              "id": "h10_3",
              "label": "3",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Are you also sorry for your sins?\nYes, I am sorry that I have sinned against God.",
              "proofs": []
            },
            {
              "id": "h10_4",
              "label": "4",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "What have you deserved from God because of your sins?\nHis wrath and displeasure, temporal death, and eternal damnation. [Romans 6:21,23]",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Romans.6_21~23",
                      "display": "Romans 6:21,23"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "h10_5",
              "label": "5",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Do you also hope to be saved?\nYes, that is my hope.",
              "proofs": []
            },
            {
              "id": "h10_6",
              "label": "6",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "In whom, then, do you trust?\nIn my dear Lord Jesus Christ.",
              "proofs": []
            },
            {
              "id": "h10_7",
              "label": "7",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Who is Christ?\nThe Son of God, true God and man.",
              "proofs": []
            },
            {
              "id": "h10_8",
              "label": "8",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "How many Gods are there?\nOnly one; but there are three persons, Father, Son, and Holy Spirit.",
              "proofs": []
            },
            {
              "id": "h10_9",
              "label": "9",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "What, then, has Christ done for you that you trust in Him?\nHe died for me, and shed His blood for me on the cross for the forgiveness of sins.",
              "proofs": []
            },
            {
              "id": "h10_10",
              "label": "10",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Did the Father also die for you?\nNo; for the Father is God only, as is the Holy Spirit. But the Son is true God and true man. He died for me and shed His blood for me.",
              "proofs": []
            },
            {
              "id": "h10_11",
              "label": "11",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "How do you know this?\nFrom the holy Gospel and from the words of the Sacrament, and by His body and blood given me as a pledge in the Sacrament.",
              "proofs": []
            },
            {
              "id": "h10_12",
              "label": "12",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "How do these words read?\n“Our Lord Jesus Christ, on the night when He was betrayed, took bread, and when He had given thanks, He broke it and gave it to the disciples and said: `Take, eat; this is My body, which is given for you. This do in remembrance of Me.’ “In the same way also He took the cup after supper, and when He had given thanks, He gave it to them saying, `Drink of it, all of you; this cup is the new testament in My blood, which is shed for you for the forgiveness of sins. This do, as often as you drink it, in remembrance of Me.”",
              "proofs": []
            },
            {
              "id": "h10_13",
              "label": "13",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Do you believe, then, that the true body and blood of Christ are in the Sacrament?\nYes, I believe it.",
              "proofs": []
            },
            {
              "id": "h10_14",
              "label": "14",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "What persuades you to believe this?\nThe word of Christ: “Take, eat, this is My body; drink of it, all of you, this is My blood.”",
              "proofs": []
            },
            {
              "id": "h10_15",
              "label": "15",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "What should we do when we eat His body and drink His blood, and in this way receive the pledge?\nWe should proclaim and remember His death and the shedding of His blood, as He taught us: “This do, as often as you drink it, in remembrance of Me.”",
              "proofs": []
            },
            {
              "id": "h10_16",
              "label": "16",
              "question": null,
