        {
          "number": "9",
          "title": "Of Free Will",
          "entries": [
            {
              "id": "c09_1",
              "label": "1",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "God hath endued the will of man with that natural liberty, that it is neither forced, nor, by any absolute necessity of nature, determined to good, or evil.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Deuteronomy.30_19",
                      "display": "Deuteronomy 30:19"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 2,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Matthew.17_12",
                      "display": "Matthew 17:12"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 3,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "James.1_14",
                      "display": "James 1:14"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "c09_2",
              "label": "2",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Man, in his state of innocency, had freedom and power to will and to do that which was good and well pleasing to God; but yet, mutably, so that he might fall from it.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Genesis.1_26",
                      "display": "Genesis 1:26"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 2,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Genesis.2_16~17",
                      "display": "Genesis 2:16,17"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 3,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Genesis.3_6",
                      "display": "Genesis 3:6"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 4,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Ecclesiastes.7_29",
                      "display": "Ecclesiastes 7:29"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "c09_3",
              "label": "3",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Man, by his fall into a state of sin, hath wholly lost all ability of will to any spiritual good accompanying salvation: so as, a natural man, being altogether averse from that good, and dead in sin, is not able, by his own strength, to convert himself, or to prepare himself thereunto.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "John.6_44~65",
                      "display": "John 6:44,65"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 2,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "John.15_5",
                      "display": "John 15:5"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
