                {
                  "id": 9,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.John.1_5",
                      "display": "1 John 1:5"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "c03_2",
              "label": "2",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Although God knoweth whatsoever may or can come to pass, upon all supposed conditions, yet hath he not decreed anything, because he foresaw it as future, or as that which would come to pass upon such conditions.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Acts.15_18",
                      "display": "Acts 15:18"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 2,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Romans.9_11~13~16~18",
                      "display": "Romans 9:11,13,16,18"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "c03_3",
              "label": "3",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "By the decree of God, for the manifestation of his glory, some men and angels are predestinated, or foreordained to eternal life through Jesus Christ, to the praise of his glorious grace; others being left to act in their sin to their just condemnation, to the praise of his glorious justice.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Matthew.25_34",
                      "display": "Matthew 25:34"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 2,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Romans.9_22~23",
                      "display": "Romans 9:22,23"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 3,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Ephesians.1_5~6",
                      "display": "Ephesians 1:5,6"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 4,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.Timothy.5_21",
                      "display": "1 Timothy 5:21"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 5,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Jude.4",
                      "display": "Jude 4"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "c03_4",
              "label": "4",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "These angels and men thus predestinated and foreordained, are particularly and unchangeably designed, and their number so certain and definite, that it cannot be either increased or diminished.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "John.13_18",
                      "display": "John 13:18"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 2,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "2.Timothy.2_19",
                      "display": "2 Timothy 2:19"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "c03_5",
              "label": "5",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Those of mankind that are predestinated to life, God, before the foundation of the world was laid, according to his eternal and immutable purpose, and the secret counsel and good pleasure of his will, hath chosen in Christ unto everlasting glory, out of his mere free grace and love, without any other thing in the creature as a condition or cause moving him thereunto.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Romans.8_30",
                      "display": "Romans 8:30"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 2,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Romans.9_13~16",
                      "display": "Romans 9:13,16"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 3,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Ephesians.1_4~9~11",
                      "display": "Ephesians 1:4,9,11"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 4,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Ephesians.2_5~12",
                      "display": "Ephesians 2:5,12"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 5,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.Thessalonians.5_9",
                      "display": "1 Thessalonians 5:9"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 6,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "2.Timothy.1_9",
                      "display": "2 Timothy 1:9"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "c03_6",
              "label": "6",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "As God hath appointed the elect unto glory, so he hath, by the eternal and most free purpose of his will, foreordained all the means thereunto; wherefore they who are elected, being fallen in Adam, are redeemed by Christ, are effectually called unto faith in Christ, by his Spirit working in due season, are justified, adopted, sanctified, and kept by his power through faith unto salvation; neither are any other redeemed by Christ, or effectually called, justified, adopted, sanctified, and saved, but the elect only.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "John.6_64",
                      "display": "John 6:64"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 2,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "John.10_26",
                      "display": "John 10:26"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 3,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "John.17_9",
                      "display": "John 17:9"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 4,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Romans.8_30",
                      "display": "Romans 8:30"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 5,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.Thessalonians.5_9~10",
                      "display": "1 Thessalonians 5:9,10"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 6,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "2.Thessalonians.2_13",
                      "display": "2 Thessalonians 2:13"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 7,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.Peter.1_2~5",
                      "display": "1 Peter 1:2,5"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "c03_7",
              "label": "7",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "The doctrine of the high mystery of predestination is to be handled with special prudence and care, that men attending the will of God revealed in his Word, and yielding obedience thereunto, may, from the certainty of their effectual vocation, be assured of their eternal election; so shall this doctrine afford matter of praise, reverence, and admiration of God, and of humility, diligence, and abundant consolation to all that sincerely obey the gospel.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Luke.10_20",
                      "display": "Luke 10:20"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 2,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Romans.11_5~6~20~33",
                      "display": "Romans 11:5,6,20,33"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 3,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Ephesians.1_6",
                      "display": "Ephesians 1:6"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 4,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.Thessalonians.1_4~5",
                      "display": "1 Thessalonians 1:4,5"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 5,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "2.Peter.1_10",
                      "display": "2 Peter 1:10"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": "4",
          "title": "Of Creation",
          "entries": [
            {
              "id": "c04_1",
              "label": "1",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "In the beginning it pleased God the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit, for the manifestation of the glory of his eternal power, wisdom, and goodness, to create or make the world, and all things therein, whether visible or invisible, in the space of six days, and all very good.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Genesis.1_31",
                      "display": "Genesis 1:31"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 2,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Job.26_13",
                      "display": "Job 26:13"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 3,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "John.1_2~3",
                      "display": "John 1:2,3"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 4,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Romans.1_20",
                      "display": "Romans 1:20"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 5,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Colossians.1_16",
                      "display": "Colossians 1:16"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 6,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Hebrews.1_2",
                      "display": "Hebrews 1:2"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "c04_2",
              "label": "2",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "After God had made all other creatures, he created man, male and female, with reasonable and immortal souls, rendering them fit unto that life to God for which they were created; being made after the image of God, in knowledge, righteousness, and true holiness; having the law of God written in their hearts, and power to fulfil it, and yet under a possibility of transgressing, being left to the liberty of their own will, which was subject to change.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Genesis.1_26~27",
                      "display": "Genesis 1:26,27"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 2,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Genesis.2_7",
                      "display": "Genesis 2:7"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 3,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Genesis.3_6",
                      "display": "Genesis 3:6"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 4,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Ecclesiastes.7_29",
                      "display": "Ecclesiastes 7:29"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 5,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Romans.2_14~15",
                      "display": "Romans 2:14,15"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "c04_3",
              "label": "3",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Besides the law written in their hearts, they received a command not to eat of the tree of knowledge of good and evil, which whilst they kept, they were happy in their communion with God, and had dominion over the creatures.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Genesis.1_26~28",
                      "display": "Genesis 1:26,28"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 2,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Genesis.2_17",
                      "display": "Genesis 2:17"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": "5",
          "title": "Of Providence",
          "entries": [
            {
              "id": "c05_1",
              "label": "1",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "God the good Creator of all things, in his infinite power and wisdom doth uphold, direct, dispose, and govern all creatures and things, from the greatest even to the least, by his most wise and holy providence, to the end for which they were created, according unto his infallible foreknowledge, and the free and immutable counsel of his own will; to the praise of the glory of his wisdom, power, justice, infinite goodness, and mercy.",
              "proofs": [
                {
