                      "osis": "1.John.2_24~25",
                      "display": "1 John 2:24,25"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": "11",
          "title": "Of Justification",
          "entries": [
            {
              "id": "c11_1",
              "label": "1",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Those whom God effectually calleth, he also freely justifieth, not by infusing righteousness into them, but by pardoning their sins, and by accounting and accepting their persons as righteous; not for anything wrought in them, or done by them, but for Christ’s sake alone; not by imputing faith itself, the act of believing, or any other evangelical obedience to them, as their righteousness; but by imputing Christ’s active obedience unto the whole law, and passive obedience in his death for their whole and sole righteousness by faith, which faith they have not of themselves; it is the gift of God.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "John.1_12",
                      "display": "John 1:12"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 2,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Romans.3_24",
                      "display": "Romans 3:24"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 3,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Romans.4_5-8",
                      "display": "Romans 4:5-8"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 4,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Romans.5_17-19",
                      "display": "Romans 5:17-19"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 5,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Romans.8_30",
                      "display": "Romans 8:30"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 6,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Ephesians.1_7",
                      "display": "Ephesians 1:7"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 7,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.Corinthians.1_30~31",
                      "display": "1 Corinthians 1:30,31"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 8,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Ephesians.2_8-10",
                      "display": "Ephesians 2:8-10"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 9,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Philippians.3_8~9",
                      "display": "Philippians 3:8,9"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "c11_2",
              "label": "2",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Faith thus receiving and resting on Christ and his righteousness, is the alone instrument of justification; yet it is not alone in the person justified, but is ever accompanied with all other saving graces, and is no dead faith, but worketh by love.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Romans.3_28",
                      "display": "Romans 3:28"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 2,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Galatians.5_6",
                      "display": "Galatians 5:6"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 3,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "James.2_17~22~26",
                      "display": "James 2:17,22,26"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "c11_3",
              "label": "3",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "Christ, by his obedience and death, did fully discharge the debt of all those that are justified; and did, by the sacrifice of himself in the blood of his cross, undergoing in their stead the penalty due unto them, make a proper, real, and full satisfaction to God’s justice in their behalf; yet, inasmuch as he was given by the Father for them, and his obedience and satisfaction accepted in their stead, and both freely, not for anything in them, their justification is only of free grace, that both the exact justice and rich grace of God might be glorified in the justification of sinners.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Isaiah.53_5~6",
                      "display": "Isaiah 53:5,6"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 2,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Romans.3_26",
                      "display": "Romans 3:26"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 3,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Romans.8_32",
                      "display": "Romans 8:32"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 4,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "2.Corinthians.5_21",
                      "display": "2 Corinthians 5:21"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 5,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Ephesians.1_6~7",
                      "display": "Ephesians 1:6,7"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 6,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Ephesians.2_7",
                      "display": "Ephesians 2:7"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 7,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Hebrews.10_14",
                      "display": "Hebrews 10:14"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 8,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.Peter.1_18~19",
                      "display": "1 Peter 1:18,19"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "c11_4",
              "label": "4",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "God did from all eternity decree to justify all the elect, and Christ did in the fullness of time die for their sins, and rise again for their justification; nevertheless, they are not justified personally, until the Holy Spirit doth in due time actually apply Christ unto them.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Romans.4_25",
                      "display": "Romans 4:25"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 2,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Galatians.3_8",
                      "display": "Galatians 3:8"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 3,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Colossians.1_21~22",
                      "display": "Colossians 1:21,22"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 4,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.Timothy.2_6",
                      "display": "1 Timothy 2:6"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 5,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Titus.3_4-7",
                      "display": "Titus 3:4-7"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 6,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.Peter.1_2",
                      "display": "1 Peter 1:2"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "c11_5",
              "label": "5",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "God doth continue to forgive the sins of those that are justified, and although they can never fall from the state of justification, yet they may, by their sins, fall under God’s fatherly displeasure; and in that condition they have not usually the light of his countenance restored unto them, until they humble themselves, confess their sins, beg pardon, and renew their faith and repentance.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Psalm.32_5",
                      "display": "Psalm 32:5"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 2,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Psalm.51",
                      "display": "Psalm 51"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 3,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Psalm.89_31-33",
                      "display": "Psalm 89:31-33"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 4,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Matthew.6_12",
                      "display": "Matthew 6:12"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 5,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Matthew.26_75",
                      "display": "Matthew 26:75"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 6,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "John.10_28",
                      "display": "John 10:28"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 7,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "1.John.1_7~9",
                      "display": "1 John 1:7,9"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "c11_6",
              "label": "6",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "The justification of believers under the Old Testament was, in all these respects, one and the same with the justification of believers under the New Testament.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Romans.4_22-24",
                      "display": "Romans 4:22-24"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": 2,
                  "refs": [
                    {
                      "osis": "Galatians.3_9",
                      "display": "Galatians 3:9"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": "12",
          "title": "Of Adoption",
          "entries": [
            {
              "id": "c12_1",
              "label": "1",
              "question": null,
              "answer": "All those that are justified, God vouchsafed, in and for the sake of his only Son Jesus Christ, to make partakers of the grace of adoption, by which they are taken into the number, and enjoy the liberties and privileges of the children of God, have his name put upon them, receive the spirit of adoption, have access to the throne of grace with boldness, are enabled to cry Abba, Father, are pitied, protected, provided for, and chastened by him as by a Father, yet never cast off, but sealed to the day of redemption, and inherit the promises as heirs of everlasting salvation.",
              "proofs": [
                {
                  "id": 1,
